<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1176–<a href="/years/1216">1216</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from 1205, succeeding his brother Baldwin I. He was the ablest of the Latin emperors, defending the empire against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and the Nicaeans and conciliating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. In 1208–1209 he suppressed the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> barons of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Henry of Flanders" width="271" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schlumberger, Gustave Léon, 1844-1929 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Flandry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, the son of Count Baldwin V of Hainaut and Countess Margaret I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. He married Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a>, in 1204; their only child died in childbirth with her mother. He also had a daughter by an unnamed mistress, who probably married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexius-slav">Alexius Slav</a>, the founder of a state in the Rhodope mountains and later a despot. Some contemporary historians say that Henry made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> and that a marriage was arranged in 1213 with Maria, Kaloyan's daughter and stepdaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boril-of-bulgaria">Tsar Boril</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Henry joined the Fourth Crusade in about 1201 and distinguished himself at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> of 1203. He was one of eight division generals in July 1203, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a>, Doge Enrico Dandolo, Louis of Blois and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor">Baldwin IX of Flanders</a>, who controlled the largest division. During the siege of 1204 he led a foraging expedition and raided a castle at Philia, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, with about 30 knights according to Robert de Clari. Emperor Alexius V Ducas laid an ambush for him, but Henry's force routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and captured a revered icon said to contain relics of Christ. He became prominent among the princes of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> captured his brother Emperor Baldwin at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> in April, Henry was chosen regent, and he succeeded to the throne when news of Baldwin's death arrived. He was crowned on 20 August. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> nobles of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> refused him allegiance, and after a two-year war Henry defeated them and confiscated the Templar castles of Ravennika and Zetouni. His reign was spent largely in struggles with Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a>, whom he later followed with Boril, defeated at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a>, and with Theodore I Lascaris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. He campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> in 1207 and in 1211 to 1212, winning the Battle of the Rhyndacus, and in 1214 agreed a truce with Theodore.</p><p>The Greek historian George Akropolites wrote that Henry, though a Frank, behaved graciously to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and made many of them his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and soldiers. In 1213 he countermanded the orders of the papal legate Pelagio Galvani, who had begun to imprison <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> clergy and close churches. Henry died on 11 June 1216, poisoned, it is said, by Oberto II of Biandrate; Gardner suggests this was at the instigation of his wife Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. His brother-in-law Peter was crowned emperor but never reached Constantinople, and his sister Yolanda ruled alone in 1217 to 1219.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry of Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Henry of Flanders</b> in 1216 there was a major deficit in leadership, and by the 1230s the city, its population drastically reduced, faced a shortage of basic foodstuffs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…took the title Prince of Achaea, but when he left for France in 1208 to claim an inheritance and died on the journey, Geoffrey I of Villehardouin took control, and at the Parliament of Ravennika in 1209 he was recognised as a vassal of the <b>Latin Emperor Henry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Latin crusader forces under <b>Henry of Flanders</b> defeated an army of the Empire of Nicaea, led by Constantine Laskaris, at Adramyttion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-19">19 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Rhyndacus, the Latin emperor <b>Henry of Flanders</b> defeated Theodore I Laskaris, founder of the Empire of Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-15">15 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexius Slav allied with the Latin Empire of Constantinople, marrying a daughter of Emperor <b>Henry of Flanders</b>, and bore the title of despot.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-melnik">Despotate of Melnik</a></li><li><blockquote>Boniface was killed by the Bulgarians of Kaloyan in 1207, leaving his infant son Demetrius under a regency; the Lombard barons' revolt of 1208-1209 was suppressed by <b>Emperor Henry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-thessalonica">Kingdom of Thessalonica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1202" aria-label="Valenciennes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry of Flanders"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5192 5816h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5192 5816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1202">What was here in 1202? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
